{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-345179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n8x{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x{y}^{″} & =8x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -2{y}^{′} & =-2\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ y & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $n=m-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\ny={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -2{y}^{′} & =-2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}-2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda -2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form ${x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$ for some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ 2(4{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficients of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c7af38e50e9de02ec12475c457fd1d741ffafef15d89d86d0ac799f67e2b2d04"} {"question" "q-345179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda =0"] ["math" "4{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda =0"] ["math" "4{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1=0"] ["math" "4{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda =0"] ["math" "8{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda =0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda =0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n-7{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+(x-5)y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series so that they use the same index of summation.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =-7{x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }7((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 2x{y}^{′} & =2x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (x-5)y & =(x-5){\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)y={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)y={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-5{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =-7({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 2x{y}^{′} & =2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-5{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (-7{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +2\\lambda -5){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (-7{\\lambda }^{2}+9\\lambda -5){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -5){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (-7{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -5){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -5){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 7{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c7af38e50e9de02ec12475c457fd1d741ffafef15d89d86d0ac799f67e2b2d04"} {"question" "q-345274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "7{\\lambda }^{2}+9\\lambda +5=0"] ["math" "7{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +5=0"] ["math" "7{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda -5=0"] ["math" "9{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +5=0"] ["math" "{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +7=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +5=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-11x{y}^{′}-(x-4)y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series so that they use the same index of summation.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =2{x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -11x{y}^{′} & =-11x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }11(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -(x-4)y & =-(x-4){\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-(x-4)y=-{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n-(x-4)y=-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}+4{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =2({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -11x{y}^{′} & =-11\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-11(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-11\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+4{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (2{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -11\\lambda +4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c7af38e50e9de02ec12475c457fd1d741ffafef15d89d86d0ac799f67e2b2d04"} {"question" "q-345275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "13{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +4=0"] ["math" "2{\\lambda }^{2}+13\\lambda +4=0"] ["math" "2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4=0"] ["math" "2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda -4=0"] ["math" "{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +2=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+(6x+1)y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (6x+1)y & ={a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(6{a}_{n-1}+{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\ (6x+1)y & ={a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(6{a}_{n-1}+{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda -1)(2\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c7af38e50e9de02ec12475c457fd1d741ffafef15d89d86d0ac799f67e2b2d04"} {"question" "q-345282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+2/3}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/2}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/3}"] ["math" "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-2/3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n8x{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-2y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 5{y}^{′} & =5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -2y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-2{a}_{n-1}){x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 5{y}^{′} & =5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -2y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-2{a}_{n-1}){x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +5\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (8\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/8}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/state.json" "file_sha256" "387fe64e012a0242bef0710cd164067938b975404cddcb56c621086f753cdecf"} {"question" "q-345405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072714/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c7af38e50e9de02ec12475c457fd1d741ffafef15d89d86d0ac799f67e2b2d04"} {"question" "q-345405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma